From db88d44e2db361c766d1a44f03f34a48c157d793 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: devgianlu <altomanigianluca@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:16:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] bthelper: let bluetoothd power the adapter on

bthelper brings the interface up with hciconfig to read the address and
send its vendor commands. That powers the adapter without the kernel's
power-on initialisation, which runs only when the adapter is powered
through the management interface. Without it Secure Simple Pairing
stays off and the kernel never registers its SMP channels, so the
adapter silently ignores LE pairing requests, and bluetoothd, finding
the adapter already powered, logs "Failed to set privacy: Rejected
(0x0b)" and never powers it on itself.

The delayed "bluetoothctl power off; power on" recovers from this only
if bluetoothd is running five seconds later. When bthelper runs from
udev before bluetoothd starts, as without systemd, it does not, and the
adapter stays in this state until the Bluetooth stack is restarted.

Take the interface down again once the helper is done, so bluetoothd
powers it on through the management interface when it starts
(AutoEnable, the default since BlueZ 5.65). If bluetoothd is already
running, power the adapter on through it right away; otherwise still
ask it once it runs, for versions without AutoEnable.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: devgianlu <altomanigianluca@gmail.com>
---
 usr/bin/bthelper | 18 +++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/usr/bin/bthelper b/usr/bin/bthelper
index 30b8e1c..d21347b 100755
--- a/usr/bin/bthelper
+++ b/usr/bin/bthelper
@@ -34,8 +34,16 @@ fi
 # Route SCO packets to the HCI interface (enables HFP/HSP)
 /usr/bin/hcitool -i $dev cmd 0x3f 0x1c 0x01 0x02 0x00 0x01 0x01 > /dev/null
 
-# Force reinitialisation to allow extra features such as Secure Simple Pairing
-# to be enabled, for currently unknown reasons. This requires bluetoothd to be
-# running, which it isn't yet. Use this kludge of forking off another shell
-# with a delay, pending a complete understanding of the issues.
-(sleep 5; /usr/bin/bluetoothctl power off; /usr/bin/bluetoothctl power on) &
+# Bringing the interface up with hciconfig skips the kernel's power-on
+# initialisation, which runs only when the adapter is powered through the
+# management interface: without it Secure Simple Pairing stays off and the
+# SMP channels, which LE pairing needs, are never registered. Leave the
+# adapter down, so that bluetoothd powers it on itself.
+/usr/bin/hciconfig $dev down
+if pidof bluetoothd > /dev/null; then
+    /usr/bin/bluetoothctl power on > /dev/null
+else
+    # bluetoothd powers the adapter on when it starts (AutoEnable, the
+    # default since BlueZ 5.65). Older versions need asking once it runs.
+    (sleep 5; /usr/bin/bluetoothctl power on > /dev/null) &
+fi
-- 
